\section{Systematization of Incidents}
\label{sec:incidents}

Table~\ref{tab:incidents} places twenty-eight documented incidents on the taxonomy of
Section~\ref{sec:taxonomy}. Appendix~\ref{app:narratives} discusses them stage by stage. Here we
reconstruct one build and integration failure in detail, because \stage{$\ast$} is the locus the
standards leave out, and then examine the 2026 wallet cluster, in which unrelated defects were
exploited by the same method.

One feature of the table deserves naming before we proceed. Our coding admits a device-level
blast radius, yet no incident in the corpus has one: every failure we record is fleet-wide or
ecosystem-wide. Selection may contribute, since device-level failures are rarely disclosed, but
there is also a structural reason. A randomness defect rarely lives in a single device, because
it lives in a design, a build
configuration, or a shared library that ships identically to everyone who receives it, so the
vulnerable population is simply the population that received the artifact. The consequence runs
through the rest of the paper: remedies that protect one device at a time are poorly matched to a
failure mode that arrives fleet-wide by construction, which is part of why the structural
defenses of Section~\ref{sec:remedies} outperform per-device hardening.

