\subsection{A build and integration failure in detail: \coldcard{}}
\label{sec:coldcard}
We reconstruct the \coldcard{} fault path from the vendor's advisory and technical backgrounder
and from Block's independent analysis~\cite{coinkite2026advisory,coinkite2026backgrounder,block2026coldcard}
(Figure~\ref{fig:coldcard}). Seed generation was meant to use only the hardware generator.
During a March 2021 migration to the libNgU library, the seed call moved from
\texttt{ckcc.rng\_bytes()} to \texttt{ngu.random.bytes()}, whose \texttt{rng\_get()} symbol
resolved to MicroPython's Yasmarang generator instead of the hardware implementation, because
both had the same signature. A preprocessor guard was meant to stop the build if the software
path was enabled, but it tested whether the controlling macro was defined, and the macro was
defined as zero, so the guard never fired~\cite{coinkite2026backgrounder,block2026coldcard}. The
fault then touches three stages. At \stage{3} the generator is non-cryptographic. At \stage{4} it
is seeded once from the low 32 bits of the chip identifier, a timer, and clock
registers~\cite{block2026coldcard}. At \stage{5} the Mk4, Q, and Mk5 reseed it with
secure-element output reduced to 32 bits, which replaces a single state
word~\cite{block2026coldcard}.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/coldcard_faultpath.pdf}
\caption{The \coldcard{} seed-generation fault path, from the vendor's backgrounder and Block's
analysis~\cite{coinkite2026backgrounder,block2026coldcard}. The intended path (green) used the
hardware generator. The shipped path (red) linked a software generator, seeded it from narrow
device state, and on later models reseeded one state word with 32 bits. The two published
estimates of the resulting search space differ, as the bottom box shows.}
\label{fig:coldcard}
\end{figure}

The two analyses agree that the seeds fall far short of 128 bits and disagree on how far. The
vendor's preliminary estimate is an effective search space of about 40 bits on the Mk2 and Mk3
and about 72 bits on the Mk4, Mk5, and Q~\cite{coinkite2026backgrounder}. Block counts at most
\bits{32} distinguishable output streams on the later models when the fallback state and the
sequence of earlier generator calls are fixed, inside a raw ceiling of about \bits{73.3}, and an
upper bound of about \bits{40.7} on the Mk2 and Mk3. Block also states that it did not test
exploitability empirically~\cite{block2026coldcard}. The difference matters for cost: a
\bits{40} search is cheap on commodity hardware, whereas \bits{72} is out of reach unless the
attacker can narrow the device state (Appendix~\ref{app:math}). The public record does not
settle which figure applies to the drained wallets. The sources we found do not attribute the
thefts to device models, and we found no public report of a seed reconstructed and matched to a
drained address. None of our conclusions rests on the lower figure. The classification as a
\stage{$\ast$} failure, the remedy analysis, and the observation that no output test could see
the fault hold under either estimate, and where a cost depends on the figure we give both. The
fixed firmware (4.2.0 for the Mk2 and Mk3, 5.6.0 for the Mk4 and Mk5, 1.5.0Q for the Q) cannot
repair a seed generated earlier, so affected users must move their funds to a new
seed~\cite{coinkite2026advisory}.

\subsection{The 2026 cluster and the public verification oracle}
\label{sec:cluster}
\coldcard{} was not the only wallet entropy failure exploited in 2026. From 27 May, attackers
drained wallets whose recovery phrases had been generated by software wallets using a weak
pseudorandom function in the CryptoJS library, a defect its disclosers named Ill Bloom. Public
reporting placed the exposure in the low thousands of addresses and the losses above five
million dollars, and the disclosers withheld the affected applications under a staged
disclosure~\cite{coinspect2026illbloom}. The method was the one used against \coldcard{}:
generate candidate phrases from the weak generator, derive their addresses, and compare them with
the chain. Milk Sad (2023) and the Trust Wallet extension (2022) had shown the same pattern with
32-bit seeds~\cite{milksad2023,ledger2023trustwallet}.

What unites these unrelated code bases is the environment. A public ledger is a free, complete,
and permanent \emph{verification oracle}. In the TLS and SSH failures, an attacker who suspected
weak key generation still had to find affected hosts and had no direct way to monetize a match.
On a blockchain every candidate seed is tested against all funded addresses at once, and balances
are public, so targets can be ranked by value before any transaction is sent. The attacker's
cost is the enumeration of the reduced seed space, which Appendix~\ref{app:math} shows is cheap
at 32 to 40 bits, while the per-victim cost is near zero. This explains the observed dynamics:
thefts arrive as sudden sweeps rather than a trickle, because an attacker who has finished
enumerating has every reason to move before disclosure. In such settings the patch-and-notify
cycle cannot protect keys that already exist.
